  "close_rendering": "Here is wisdom. Let the one who has a mind calculate the number of the beast, for it is a number of a man, and its number is six hundred sixty-six.",
  "whats_happening": "The passage about the second beast ends by turning to the reader and setting a task rather than giving an explanation. The number is written as three ordinary words, not in digits. The verse calls it 'a number of a man' without saying who or what is meant. This is one of two places in the book that call for wisdom, and the only one that asks the reader to calculate.",
  "key_words": [
    {
      "surface": "ψηφισάτω",
      "lemma": "ψηφίζω",
      "gloss": "let him calculate",
      "note": "A command in the form 'let him…'. The verb means to count up or work out a total.",
      "strongs": "G5585"
    },
    {
      "surface": "ἀνθρώπου",
      "lemma": "ἄνθρωπος",
      "gloss": "of a man",
      "note": "'Of a man', with no 'the', which leaves open whether a particular person or people in general is meant.",
      "strongs": "G0444"
    },
    {
